## 6. Detailed migration nuances

### 6.1 Decorators → function handlers (the core change)

Delete `routing-controllers` controllers. Each controller class becomes a **module exporting a `Hono` sub-app** (or a function that registers routes on a passed-in app). Map the param decorators to direct request access:

| routing-controllers | Hono |
|---|---|
| `@JsonController('/users')` + method decorators | a `new Hono()` sub-app mounted at `/users` via `app.route('/users', userRoutes)` |
| `@Get('/:id')` / `@Post()` / `@Put()` / `@Delete()` | `userRoutes.get('/:id', ...)` etc. |
| `@ReqPathParam('id')` / `@Param('id')` | `c.req.param('id')` |
| `@ReqQueryParam('q')` / `@QueryParam('q')` | `c.req.query('q')` |
| `@ReqHeaderParam('h')` / `@HeaderParam('h')` | `c.req.header('h')` |
| `@Body()` | `await c.req.json()` (or the parsed body from a validator middleware) |
| `@QueryParams()` / `@Params()` | `c.req.query()` / `c.req.param()` with no key (whole object) |
| `@Req()` / `@Res()` | `c.req` / `c` (Hono's context wraps both request and response) |
| `@CurrentUser()` | a value set on context by an auth middleware, read via `c.get('user')` (Hono's typed context store, or `AsyncLocalStorage`) |
| `@UploadedFile()` / `@UploadedFiles()` | `hono/body-limit` + manual `c.req.parseBody()` for multipart |
| `@HttpCode(201)` | `c.json(body, 201)` |
| return value (auto-serialized) | explicit `return c.json(value)` — **Hono does not auto-serialize a returned plain object the way Fastify/`routing-controllers` did; every handler must call `c.json()`/`c.text()`/etc. explicitly** |
| throwing `NotFoundError`, `BadRequestError`, … | `throw new HTTPException(404, { message: 'user not found' })` from `hono/http-exception` (§6.8) |

**Before/after shape:**
```ts
// BEFORE
@JsonController('/users')
export class UserController {
  constructor(private svc: UserService) {}        // typedi injection
  @Get('/:id')
  @HttpCode(200)
  async getUser(@ReqPathParam('id') id: string,
                @ReqQueryParam('full') full: boolean) {
    const u = await this.svc.find(id, full);
    if (!u) throw new NotFoundError('user not found');
    return u;
  }
}

// AFTER — sub-app module, function handler, validator middleware
import { Hono } from 'hono';
import { HTTPException } from 'hono/http-exception';
import { zValidator } from '@hono/zod-validator';
import { z } from 'zod';
import { makeUserService } from './user-service';

const paramsSchema = z.object({ id: z.string() });
const querySchema = z.object({ full: z.coerce.boolean().optional() });

export const userRoutes = new Hono();
const svc = makeUserService(/* deps */);

userRoutes.get(
  '/:id',
  zValidator('param', paramsSchema),
  zValidator('query', querySchema),
  async (c) => {
    const { id } = c.req.valid('param');
    const { full } = c.req.valid('query');
    const u = await svc.find(id, full ?? false);
    if (!u) throw new HTTPException(404, { message: 'user not found' });
    return c.json(u, 200);
  }
);

// top-level composition file
app.route('/users', userRoutes);
```
Note: unlike the Fastify/`fast-json-stringify` model, there is **no automatic response serialization from a schema** — the `response` validation (if you choose to validate outgoing bodies at all) is a separate, optional concern from what actually gets sent. Don't assume a "response schema" buys you a performance win here the way it did in the Fastify plan; it buys you a contract check, which is still valuable but is a different claim.

### 6.2 Validation: `class-validator` → TypeBox/Zod (library choice open)

This decision was **explicitly left open** in prior planning and still needs to be made before §4 step 4 starts. Whichever is chosen, preserve constraints exactly:

| class-validator | Zod (if chosen) | TypeBox (if chosen) |
|---|---|---|
| `@IsString()` | `z.string()` | `Type.String()` |
| `@IsInt()` / `@IsNumber()` | `z.number().int()` / `z.number()` | `Type.Integer()` / `Type.Number()` |
| `@IsBoolean()` | `z.coerce.boolean()` (querystring) | `Type.Boolean()` + coercion |
| `@IsEmail()` | `z.string().email()` | `Type.String({ format: 'email' })` |
| `@Min(n)` / `@Max(n)` | `.min(n)` / `.max(n)` | `minimum` / `maximum` |
| `@Length(a,b)` / `@MinLength` / `@MaxLength` | `.min()` / `.max()` on string | `minLength` / `maxLength` |
| `@IsEnum(E)` | `z.nativeEnum(E)` | `Type.Enum(E)` |
| `@IsOptional()` | `.optional()` | `Type.Optional(...)` |
| `@ValidateNested()` + `@Type(() => X)` | nested `z.object({...})` | nested `Type.Object({...})` |
| `@IsArray()` + element decorators | `z.array(itemSchema)` | `Type.Array(itemSchema)` |
| custom validators (`FutureDate`, `PastDate`, `BetweenDates`, `ValidDate`) | `.refine()` / `.superRefine()` | custom TypeBox `Kind` + AJV keyword, or a wrapping refinement |

**Custom validators**: reproduce the exact semantics as refinements/custom keywords, not loosened approximations. If a validator encoded a business rule (e.g. "date must be after today"), unit-test it against the old behavior before considering it ported.

Whichever library is chosen, wire it through `@hono/zod-validator` (Zod) or an equivalent TypeBox validator middleware so `c.req.valid(...)` is available in handlers — do not hand-roll ad hoc validation per route.

### 6.3 Middleware classes → Hono middleware functions

Express and Hono both use an `(c, next) => ...` / `async (c, next) => { ...; await next(); ... }` style — this is a **much smaller conceptual jump** than the Fastify staged-lifecycle model was. There's no `onRequest`/`preParsing`/`preHandler` distinction; ordering is purely registration order plus before/after-`next()` placement:

| Middleware (current) | Hono equivalent | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `ContextMiddleware` (request context / correlation) | a middleware that opens an `AsyncLocalStorage` run() before calling `next()` | establish the store as early as possible in the chain; see §6.4 |
| auth / token validation | a middleware that sets `c.set('user', ...)` before `next()` | scope to the routes/sub-apps that need it, not globally, if the old middleware wasn't global |
| request/response logging middleware | mostly **removed** → `hono/logger`, or a thin wrapper that logs after `await next()` | keep only fields the built-in logger doesn't cover |
| `TimeoutMiddleware` | `hono/timeout` built-in, or a custom `AbortController`-based wrapper | confirm behavior on already-streaming responses (SSE/WS) — timeout middleware should generally exclude those routes |
| `NoCacheMiddleware` | a middleware that sets `Cache-Control` headers on `c.res` after `await next()` | equivalent to Express's response-header mutation pattern |
| body/raw capture | a custom body-reading step before validation, on signature-verifying routes only | §6.11 |

Since Hono's model is closer to Express's than Fastify's staged lifecycle was, **pay extra attention to before-`next()` vs after-`next()` placement** — it's easy to accidentally collapse two middlewares that had distinct "before" and "after" responsibilities in the old chain into one that only does one or the other. Verify effective order matches the old Express chain for any route where ordering had observable effects (e.g. auth before rate-limit vs after).

### 6.4 DI removal (`typedi`) + request context — settled, not optional

Unlike the earlier Fastify-era planning (which left "keep typedi as a standalone container" open), this migration **removes `typedi` entirely**. There is no DI container in the target state.

- **Singletons** (`@Service()` with no request state) → plain module-level singletons or factory functions, instantiated once at bootstrap and imported where needed.
- **Request-scoped state** (correlation IDs, per-request user/tenant, anything `ContextMiddleware` stored on the request) → **`AsyncLocalStorage`**, established in the earliest middleware in the chain (§6.3) and read anywhere downstream via a small accessor module (e.g. `getRequestContext()`).
- Hono's own `c.set()`/`c.get()` typed context store can hold *handler-local* values scoped to the current request's middleware chain, but it does not propagate into code called outside the Hono context object (e.g. deep service-layer functions) the way `AsyncLocalStorage` does — use `AsyncLocalStorage` for anything that needs to reach code that doesn't have `c` in scope, and `c.set()`/`c.get()` for things read only within handlers/middleware.
- **Preserve service lifetimes** — turning a request-scoped service into a singleton (or vice versa) is a behavior change and a potential cross-request data leak. Flag any service whose scope is ambiguous rather than guessing (§9).

### 6.5 (reserved)

### 6.6 GraphQL: Apollo Server 4 → Apollo Server 5 via `apollo-server-integration-hono`

- This is a **forced two-part change**: Apollo Server 4 is end-of-life (Jan 2026), so the upgrade to Apollo Server 5 (Node ≥20.0.0 — already satisfied by §2.2's floor) needs to happen regardless of the framework migration. Do it as a distinct, tracked step so an Apollo-version regression isn't conflated with a Hono-integration bug.
- Install `apollo-server-integration-hono` — **this is a community-maintained package, not an official Apollo or Hono integration.** Before adopting it: check its maintenance activity, open issues, and whether it tracks current Apollo Server 5 minor releases. If it looks under-maintained, flag this as a risk rather than silently proceeding (§9).
- Construct `ApolloServer` as usual and use the package's `startServerAndCreateHonoHandler(server, { context })` to produce a Hono-compatible handler, mounted at the existing GraphQL path.
- `await apollo.start()` before wiring the handler, same as before.
- Context: provide a context function shaped for Hono's context object (check the integration package's exact signature — it typically receives the Hono `Context` rather than raw `request`/`reply`). Port whatever the old context builder did (auth, loaders, tenant) into this function.
- **Subscriptions:** as before, Apollo Server does **not** handle WS subscriptions itself. If the current setup has GraphQL subscriptions, that's separate `graphql-ws` + `@hono/node-ws` wiring — **do not assume it carries over; flag it** (§9).

### 6.7 WebSockets (`ws` + decorators) and SSE

- **WebSockets:** `@hono/node-ws`'s `createNodeWebSocket({ app })` returns `{ injectWebSocket, upgradeWebSocket }`. Replace `WebSocketDecorators` with `app.get('/path', upgradeWebSocket((c) => ({ onMessage, onClose, onOpen, ... })))`, and call `injectWebSocket(server)` on the `@hono/node-server`-returned server instance after `serve()`. Note the connection/lifecycle API (`onOpen`/`onMessage`/`onClose`) differs from raw `ws`; port handlers accordingly and preserve auth by validating **before** the upgrade (in middleware ahead of the `upgradeWebSocket` route), since headers can become immutable once the upgrade helper runs.
- **SSE (`SSE.ts`) is not WebSockets.** It's a long-lived `text/event-stream` HTTP response. Use `hono/streaming`'s `streamSSE(c, async (stream) => { ... })` helper, which handles the headers and write loop. Keep the event format and heartbeat behavior identical. Migrate SSE and WS as **separate** concerns.
- If both CORS/secure-headers middleware and the WebSocket upgrade route interact on the same path, test explicitly — Hono's own docs flag header-mutation conflicts between middleware and `upgradeWebSocket()`.

### 6.8 Error handling + error-shape contract

- `routing-controllers` had `HttpError` subclasses and a global error handler producing a specific JSON error shape. **Capture that exact shape first** (status code + body) from the current code/tests.
- In Hono, set `app.onError((err, c) => { ... })` to reproduce the same body for the same conditions. Map each old error class → a thrown `HTTPException` (from `hono/http-exception`) or a custom error class caught in `onError`.
- **Validation errors:** whatever validator middleware you choose (Zod/TypeBox) has its own default error shape on failure — override its error handler (`zValidator`'s optional hook argument, or the TypeBox validator's equivalent) so it matches `class-validator`'s old body exactly. This is a contract item, not cosmetic.
- Ensure thrown errors still get traced/reported to APM (§6.10) — confirm the APM agent's Node/HTTP-level instrumentation still captures unhandled and `onError`-caught exceptions the way Express-level middleware errors used to.

### 6.9 Logging: drop `morgan` → `hono/logger` or Pino

- `hono/logger` is a minimal built-in request logger; it likely won't match the field set the old `morgan`+custom middleware produced. Decide between the built-in logger (simpler, fewer fields) and continuing with Pino wired in as custom middleware (more control, matches the observability stack's existing use of structured logging elsewhere).
- Set request-id generation so it derives from the incoming correlation header if one exists, and wire it to the OTel trace ID so logs ↔ traces correlate — same requirement as before, framework-independent.
- **Banking/PII redaction is mandatory.** If using Pino, keep its `redact` config; if using `hono/logger`, you likely need a wrapping layer since it doesn't have built-in redaction. Inventory what the current logging exposed (auth headers, tokens, account numbers, PANs, sensitive body fields) and ensure the new logs are at least as redacted.
- Reproduce any log fields downstream systems parse (request id, latency, status, route). Remove `morgan` only after parity is confirmed.

### 6.10 Observability preservation (higher-risk area than the Fastify plan)

- **Init order:** the OTel SDK (`@opentelemetry/sdk-node`) and instrumentations must still be **started before the app/server code is imported/instantiated**, or auto-instrumentation hooks won't attach. Keep this in a tracing bootstrap loaded first. Verify this didn't get reordered during the migration.
- **There is no `@opentelemetry/instrumentation-hono` equivalent to what Fastify had.** `auto-instrumentations-node`'s `instrumentation-http` will still instrument the underlying Node `http`/`http2` server that `@hono/node-server` runs on, which gets you request-level spans — but route-level naming/attribution (e.g. `GET /users/:id` rather than the raw path) is **not automatic** the way `instrumentation-fastify` provided it. Plan to add a thin middleware that reads the matched route pattern (Hono exposes this on the context) and sets it as a span attribute/name, and treat this as a build-and-verify task, not an assumption.
- `elastic-apm-node`'s auto-instrumentation for Express/Fastify won't apply the same way to Hono; confirm empirically whether transactions are still named usefully by route, and add manual transaction naming if not.
- `prom-client`: keep the existing registry/metrics; there's no `fastify-metrics`-style wrapper for Hono, so HTTP metrics (request counts, latency histograms by route/status) need a small custom middleware that increments/observes on `onResponse`-equivalent (i.e., after `await next()`). **Preserve metric names and labels** so dashboards/alerts keep working.

### 6.11 Raw body / webhook signature verification (gotcha — unchanged in spirit)

If `raw-body` was used to verify webhook/HMAC signatures (common for payment/bank callbacks), any JSON-parsing step before your handler destroys the raw bytes needed for signature verification. On signature-verifying routes specifically, read the body as a raw buffer/text (`await c.req.arrayBuffer()` or `.text()`) **before** any JSON-parsing validator middleware runs, verify the signature against those raw bytes, and only then parse. Losing the raw body silently breaks signature checks — verify these routes explicitly and don't let a global JSON-parsing validator run ahead of the raw read on them.

### 6.12 Routing composition (no autoload)

There is no `@fastify/autoload`/`require-all` equivalent. Build an explicit composition root: each former controller's module exports a `Hono` sub-app; a single top-level file `app.route(prefix, subApp)`s all of them together. Preserve the old route prefixes (the former `routePrefix` / controller base paths) exactly in this composition file so URLs don't change. Treat this file as the canonical route map going forward — it's less "magic" than the old globbing setup, which is a net positive for auditability, but it means every new route module must be remembered and wired in explicitly (add a lint/test check that fails if a routes file exists but isn't mounted).

### 6.13 Dual CJS + ESM preservation (the accommodation from §2.2)

- Because Hono core ships ESM-only, the package's **CJS build's internal use of `hono`/`@hono/node-server`/`@hono/node-ws` will rely on Node's synchronous `require(esm)` support**, available from Node `20.19.0`/`22.12.0` onward. Raise `engines.node` accordingly and document this explicitly in the migration/changelog notes — this is a real, if narrow, floor-raise for consumers, not a cosmetic version bump.
- Confirm none of Hono's dependency graph relies on **top-level await** anywhere reachable from your import path — `require(esm)` only works when the ESM graph can resolve synchronously; a top-level `await` anywhere in that graph breaks it and forces you back to dynamic `import()` (async) in the CJS entry, which is a bigger consumer-facing change. Verify this rather than assuming it.
- Keep the rest of the package's existing dual CJS+ESM build setup (the `exports` map, `main`/`module` fields) as-is; only the Node floor changes.
- Before merging, **build and import the package both ways** on the new minimum Node version — a `require()` smoke test and an `import` smoke test — to prove dual consumption still works. Do not add any *additional* ESM-only dependency beyond Hono's own ecosystem without flagging it (§9).

